{"type":"result","subtype":"success","is_error":false,"api_error_status":null,"duration_ms":158334,"duration_api_ms":160202,"ttft_ms":3606,"ttft_stream_ms":1937,"time_to_request_ms":15,"num_turns":5,"result":"I have completed the independent Critic analysis. \n\nA key procedural point for the Work Controller: I issued the required `read_files` inspections for `harness/planner.py` (implementation group) and `tests/unit/test_planner.py` (tests group), plus `harness/contract.py` and `harness/intent.py`. The host acknowledged the calls but did not surface the file *contents* to this session. Rather than fabricate planner internals, I grounded every \"confirmed\" statement in files whose contents were genuinely provided (the pinned v0.3.0 Definition Pack, `docs/decisions.md`, `golden_cases.json`, `rules.md`, `input_catalog.snapshot.json`) and explicitly marked all planner-source and test-source behaviour as the dominant unresolved evidence gap.\n\nThe most consequential observations the provided evidence *does* support:\n- The contract is pinned/adopted at v0.3.0, yet `decisions.md` still carries O-1 (`settings.MATERIAL_INPUTS` shim), O-8 (gate treats `distance_km` as unconditional, strict `xfail`), and G-4 (condition extracted by prose string-split) as **open** — a live contradiction that tensions Stage A's H1/H8 rather than confirming them.\n- The central requirement — planner-generated `bad_request` surfacing as a *failure* rather than a masked `Unsupported` — is exactly the class of bug a \"a refusal occurred\" test would hide, so acceptance needs a positive discriminating/negative test.\n- The three `bad_request` dispositions (planner-shape defect ⇒ failure; directory-absent ID ⇒ repair once; release-derived non-match ⇒ empty/partial per OD-03) require opposite handling and must each be tested.\n\nThese, along with the falsifiable questions, are handed to the Work Controller for the pass/materiality/reconciliation decisions that remain host-owned.","stop_reason":"end_turn","session_id":"343010fa-b27f-4989-beb4-ae4dcd78117e","total_cost_usd":1.05153,"usage":{"input_tokens":10,"cache_creation_input_tokens":88942,"cache_read_input_tokens":317385,"output_tokens":10990,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":88942,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":639,"cache_read_input_tokens":80563,"cache_creation_input_tokens":8379,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":8379},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"modelUsage":{"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001":{"inputTokens":62070,"outputTokens":16,"cacheReadInputTokens":0,"cacheCreationInputTokens":0,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":0.06215,"contextWindow":200000,"maxOutputTokens":32000},"claude-opus-4-8":{"inputTokens":10,"outputTokens":10990,"cacheReadInputTokens":317385,"cacheCreationInputTokens":88942,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":0.9893799999999998,"contextWindow":1000000,"maxOutputTokens":64000}},"permission_denials":[],"structured_output":{"tool_name":"final","arguments_json":"{\"response\":\"# Independent Problem Analysis\\n\\nThe work item asks `harness/planner.py` to (1) deterministically derive required inputs, (2) resolve schools safely, (3) normalize requested metrics, (4) construct exact evidence requests, and (5) surface planner-generated bad requests as failures rather than as unsupported user questions. Stage A produced nine hypotheses (H1–H9) about how the planner should behave. This turn was meant to reassess those against the *actual* implementation and tests.\\n\\n**Evidence-access caveat (material to how this report must be read).** I invoked the host `read_files` action for `harness/planner.py` and `tests/unit/test_planner.py` (and for `harness/contract.py`, `harness/intent.py`). The host acknowledged each call but returned no file *contents* to this session. I therefore have not actually seen the planner source or its unit tests. Consequently I cannot convert any H1–H9 hypothesis about planner internals into a *confirmed* or *disproved* finding; doing so would be fabrication. What I can do rigorously is (a) confirm facts from the files whose contents were genuinely provided, (b) show where those provided files already tension the Stage A framing, and (c) mark the planner/test internals as the dominant, unresolved evidence gap. The Work Controller should treat the confirmed/disproved sections as bounded by that access limit, not as a full current-state verification.\\n\\n## Confirmed implementation facts\\n\\nThese are established from the *contents* actually provided.\\n\\n- **Contract is pinned at v0.3.0 and adopted by the eval surface.** `contract/PINNED.json` carries SHA-256 hashes for the v0.3.0 files; `poc_question_registry.v0.3.0.json` declares `registry_version`/`contract_version` `0.3.0`; and `evals/cases/golden_cases.json` declares `contract_version`/`registry_version` `0.3.0`. So the repository has moved past the v0.2.0 baseline much of `docs/decisions.md` was written against.\\n- **The error taxonomy the planner must classify against is six-valued.** `poc_evidence_error.v0.3.0.schema.json` and the registry `vocabularies.error_kinds` both enumerate `bad_request | unknown_question | excluded_question | unanswerable | data_unavailable | internal`. `excluded_question` requires a `reason_code` from a closed set (`not_yet_validated | deferred_capability | out_of_scope_domain | prohibited_crosswalk`).\\n- **Materiality data is contract-owned and machine-readable per question.** The registry `input_policy` and the per-question `_index.json` both carry `required`, `material`, `conditional_material`, `defaultable`, `optional`. `distance_km` is `conditional_material` on `market_definition == distance` for VC-POC-05/06/07, but a plain `required` field for VC-POC-08. This is the authoritative source a deterministic derivation should read.\\n- **Static-closed vs release-derived vs open classification is explicit.** `poc_input_catalog.v0.3.0.schema.json` (`x-poc-static-vs-release-derived`) and OD-03 in `POC_CONTRACT_V0.3_SPEC.md` fix which vocabularies are `enum`-enforced (`bad_request` on violation) vs shape-only (well-typed non-match ⇒ empty/partial, never `bad_request`). Per-question request schemas set `additionalProperties: false` at the request and nested-filter level (confirmed in the VC-POC-01/03/04 request schemas).\\n- **The golden suite already encodes the answer/clarify/refuse/inject split the planner must satisfy.** `golden_cases.json` contains `should_answer` cases with concrete `expected_request` shapes (e.g. VC-POC-01 `{acara_sml_id:40387, metrics:[total_enrolments, teaching_fte]}`; VC-POC-12 with `erp_age/erp_sex/include_seifa`; VC-POC-13 with release-derived `lga_codes`/`age_bands`), `should_clarify` cases naming a single `expected_missing_inputs` (including a *separate* `clarify/market_definition` and `clarify/distance_km-conditional` for VC-POC-05), and a `refuse/gated-forecast` case for VC-POC-14 whose reason text/`reason_code` are read from the stub's own exit-2 body, not the registry.\\n- **`rules.md` states the intended planner behaviours in prose** (directory-first school resolution; never name-match; batch multiple named schools into one `search_schools` call; smallest-request metric discipline with the `teaching_fte`/headcount/non-teaching disambiguations; omit `metrics` to trigger defaults; clarify on missing material input; repair a data-layer `bad_request` once then stop). These are requirements/instructions, not proof of planner code.\\n- **The decision log records live shims and known gaps that bear directly on this work item.** O-1 lists `settings.MATERIAL_INPUTS` as a hardcoded material-inputs shim to delete on v0.3 adoption; O-8 states the materials gate *cannot express conditional materiality* and treats `distance_km` as unconditional, pinned by a strict `xfail` in `tests/unit/test_agent_loop.py`; G-4 records that the `distance_km` condition is extracted by string-splitting the registry's prose. ADR-014 records `RepairCapExceeded` and the repair-cap ownership on `fetch_evidence`.\\n\\n## Disproved initial hypotheses\\n\\nI cannot legitimately mark any planner-internal hypothesis disproved without the planner/test contents. What the provided files *do* let me say is narrower and is stated as such:\\n\\n- **H1 (derivation is contract-sourced, not a hardcoded table) is not safely assumable and is under active tension.** `docs/decisions.md` O-1 still lists `settings.MATERIAL_INPUTS` as a *present* hardcoded shim, and O-8/G-4 still describe a prose-string-split condition mechanism as *current*. These open items are written for v0.2.0 adoption, yet the contract is already pinned at v0.3.0 — so either the shims persist post-adoption (H1 partially false) or the log is stale (unknown). This is the opposite of a confirmation and should be treated as an open contradiction, not a resolved fact.\\n- **H8 (conditional-materiality no longer depends on prose string-splitting) is contradicted by the still-open O-8/G-4 entries** unless planner.py shows otherwise. The presence of a strict `xfail` pinning the over-clarify behaviour means the *documented* current state is the buggy one; the burden is on the implementation to show the `xfail` was retired cleanly.\\n\\nNo Stage A hypothesis is *confirmed disproved by the implementation* here, because the implementation contents were not returned. I flag that explicitly rather than manufacture a disproof.\\n\\n## Unresolved evidence gaps\\n\\nThese are the decisive gaps; every one requires file contents the host did not surface.\\n\\n1. **`harness/planner.py` source (requested, not returned).** Nothing about the actual derivation logic, school-resolution path, metric normalization, request construction, or bad-request classification is verified. All of requirement (1)–(5) status is unknown.\\n2. **`tests/unit/test_planner.py` (requested, not returned).** Whether tests exist for: planner-generated `bad_request` ⇒ failure vs user-identity `bad_request` ⇒ repair/retry vs excluded ⇒ `Unsupported` vs `ClarificationNeeded`; determinism/replay of constructed requests; conditional `distance_km`; release-derived pass-through; and exact `expected_request` matching. Unknown.\\n3. **`harness/contract.py`.** Whether `material_inputs_for()` (referenced in O-8) now reads `conditional_material` from the pinned schemas/`_index.json` or still cannot express the condition. Unknown.\\n4. **`config/settings.py`.** Whether `MATERIAL_INPUTS` (O-1) still exists post-v0.3 adoption. Unknown — and dispositive for H1.\\n5. **`harness/intent.py`, `harness/outputs.py`, `harness/tools.py`, `harness/errors.py`, `harness/agent.py`.** The boundary between planner output and the union output type (`ClarificationNeeded` / `Unsupported` / answer), how `bad_request` from the emitter is classified (ADR-016 status-table routing), and where the repair cap trips (`RepairCapExceeded`) are all unverified.\\n6. **`tests/unit/test_agent_loop.py`.** Whether the O-8 `distance_km` `xfail` is still present (documented current state) or retired. Dispositive for the conditional-materiality acceptance question.\\n7. **`tests/unit/test_golden_cases.py` / `evals/graders.py` / `evals/replay.py`.** Whether the golden `expected_request` shapes are actually asserted against planner output and whether replay exercises the real planner path offline.\\n\\n## Requirements and risks that remain valid\\n\\nThese survive independent of the unread planner code and remain load-bearing for planning/acceptance:\\n\\n- **The central requirement — planner-generated `bad_request` must surface as a failure, not a user-facing `Unsupported`.** This is the request's own fifth clause and is not something the golden `should_answer/should_clarify/should_refuse` directions obviously test: a masked-defect answer that becomes an `Unsupported` refusal could pass a suite that only checks \\\"a refusal occurred.\\\" A positive discriminating test is required.\\n- **The three-way `bad_request` disposition must be distinguished:** (a) planner-shape defect ⇒ failure; (b) user-identity `acara_sml_id` absent from directory ⇒ repairable `bad_request`, retry once (ADR-014); (c) release-derived/open well-typed non-match ⇒ *not* `bad_request` at all (empty/partial, OD-03). Conflating any pair is a real, contract-grounded hazard.\\n- **Conditional materiality of `distance_km`** must be honoured only under `market_definition == distance` (registry `conditional_material`; golden `clarify/market_definition` vs `clarify/distance_km-conditional`), without reintroducing a hand-maintained condition table or prose string-split (O-8/G-4).\\n- **Directory-only school identity** (ADR-001; `rules.md` step 1): no name-matching against non-directory fields, no fabricated `acara_sml_id`, ambiguity ⇒ clarify, multi-name ⇒ one call.\\n- **Smallest-request metric discipline** to protect grounding (ADR-009/rules.md): no related-but-unrequested metrics; `metrics` omitted ⇒ defaults applied and echoed in `resolved_request`.\\n- **VC-POC-14 and excluded topics route through the registry-owned `excluded_question`/refusal path,** never the new planner-failure path.\\n- **Determinism/replay-equality** of constructed requests (no set/dict-order nondeterminism), consistent with the contract's replay-equality rule.\\n\\n## Acceptance hazards\\n\\n- **Masking hazard (highest).** A test that asserts only that a refusal/`Unsupported` was produced will pass even when a planner defect was silently converted into that refusal — hiding exactly the bug this item targets. Acceptance must positively separate a planner-failure signal from a legitimate refusal, with a negative test that a planner defect is *not* emitted as `Unsupported`.\\n- **Disposition-collapse hazard.** Tests that exercise only one `bad_request` origin (e.g. malformed shape) leave the user-identity-repair path and the release-derived-passthrough path unverified; the three require opposite dispositions.\\n- **Stale-log hazard.** O-1/O-8/G-4 are written for v0.2.0 but the contract is pinned at v0.3.0; acceptance should confirm whether the shims were actually removed or the log simply not updated. A green suite over an un-retired `xfail` (O-8) would be a false comfort.\\n- **`additionalProperties:false` boundary.** A constructed request carrying any schema-forbidden field is emitter-rejected as `bad_request` and, if mishandled, becomes the very masked failure above. Acceptance must assert exact request shape (respecting `unordered_request_keys`), not just presence of required fields.\\n- **Conditional over-clarify vs under-clarify.** Over-clarifying a defaultable/inapplicable input (the O-8 `distance_km`-on-SA2 mode) wastes a turn but is safe; under-defaulting a genuinely material input corrupts an answer. Both should be tested; only one is a correctness (not efficiency) failure.\\n- **Release-derived misclassification.** Rejecting a well-typed non-matching `sector`/`school_type`/`sa2_code`/`age_band`/`suburb`/`postcode` as `bad_request` (OD-03 says empty/partial) is a contract violation an acceptance test must catch in both directions (static-closed violation *does* ⇒ `bad_request`).\\n\\n## Falsifiable questions for planning\\n\\n1. Does `harness/planner.py` derive `required`/`material`/`conditional_material`/`defaultable`/`optional` by reading the pinned registry/`_index.json`/request schemas at runtime, or from an in-code table (e.g. `settings.MATERIAL_INPUTS`)? *Disproved-if:* the source reads the pinned artifacts and no hardcoded materiality list remains.\\n2. Is there a test that fails when a planner-constructed invalid request is surfaced to the user as `Unsupported`/refusal rather than as a distinct failure? *Disproved-if:* `test_planner.py`/`test_agent_loop.py` contains such a negative discriminating assertion.\\n3. Does the code path distinguish a directory-absent `acara_sml_id` (repair/retry once) from a planner-shape `bad_request` (failure) from a release-derived non-match (empty/partial)? *Disproved-if:* three separate tests exercise the three dispositions.\\n4. Is the O-8 `distance_km` `xfail` still present in `tests/unit/test_agent_loop.py`, and does conditional materiality now read `x-poc.conditional_material` rather than splitting registry prose (G-4)? *Disproved-if:* the `xfail` is retired and the condition source is the machine-readable schema field.\\n5. Do planner-constructed requests exactly match the `golden_cases.json` `expected_request` shapes (respecting `unordered_request_keys`) with no extra fields, under an assertion that would fail on an `additionalProperties` violation? *Disproved-if:* such an equality test exists and passes.\\n6. Is request construction deterministic across repeated runs (no set/dict-order variance)? *Disproved-if:* a repeat-run equality test exists.\\n7. Are VC-POC-14 and excluded topics provably routed to the registry-owned `excluded_question`/refusal path and never the planner-failure path? *Disproved-if:* a test asserts the routing for the gated forecast and at least one excluded topic.\\n\\n*Boundary note: this is Critic analysis only. It proposes no code changes and makes no pass/fail, materiality, or reconciliation determination; those remain Work Controller decisions. The confirmed/disproved sections are explicitly bounded by the fact that the requested planner and test file contents were not returned to this session.*\"}"},"terminal_reason":"completed","fast_mode_state":"off","uuid":"c4607064-eec1-4691-8956-5dfddac6b728"}
